sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, one Swamp run, Ansible phases for idempotent Node state (rule 22). docs/reference/node-provisioning.md#remove-a-node.
  %% status: reviewed
  %% command: node:remove
  %% kind: node.remove
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane P icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as Target Node<br/>reached over ssh
  participant P as Fleet Nodes<br/>hub · DNS
  CLI->>GW: node:remove NODE
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit node:remove NODE`
  %%> - syntax: `<node> [--force] [--offline] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Node ID
  %%> - `--force` and `--offline` are consent; the Gateway requires `--force` for an offline removal or a Node with roles
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, mark removing
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node, and the target is not the caller
  %%> - the Gateway, VPN and control Nodes are protected
  %%> - no Instances, Database servers, Schedules, Routes or user firewall rules remain; an online removal also has no Processes and no role dependents
  %%> - a task VM host is drained and its task VM settings cleared
  %%> - no other removal runs for the Node (`node.removal_busy`)
  %%> Builds:
  %%> - the pinned SSH targets, the publication changes for the fleet, and the exact managed paths
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the Node is recorded as `removing`, and a task is recorded with the full intent
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-node-remove
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task ID, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>N: Reach the Node<br/>SSH argv
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - probes the Node with its approved identity
  %%> - an unreachable Node without `--offline` fails the run here (`node.removal_incomplete`); with `--offline` only its registry membership and fleet publications are removed
  SW->>N: Shed the Node's roles<br/>native child workflow
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes each mutable role and withdraws its publications on other Nodes; protected roles stay with their owning lifecycle
  %%> - an unreachable Node's own services are not touched
  opt Linux Node reachable
    SW->>N: Remove Orbit from the Node<br/>Ansible phase: remove
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - restores public SSH, removes the services, files, configuration, containers and firewall rules Orbit created, and the agent and worker footprint
    %%> - withdraws residual catalog-owned task-host DHCP/DNS input rules through the shared Ansible phase before removing the management keys
    %%> Retained:
    %%> - packages, users, user data, package managers and unrelated SSH keys
    SW->>N: Check the removal<br/>Ansible phase: check
    %%> Why before the keys:
    %%> - the check needs SSH, and a failed run must stay reachable for the rerun
    SW->>N: Revoke management keys<br/>Ansible phase: keys
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - removes only the admitted Orbit management keys; the control key goes last, and nothing connects to the Node afterwards
  end
  opt macOS or iPadOS peer reachable
    SW->>N: Remove the peer's keys<br/>SSH argv
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - revokes the admitted keys; the account, tools and host tunnel stay
  end
  SW->>P: Remove the hub peer<br/>native child workflow
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review: deletes this Node's own peer file on the hub, then loads the files that remain with `wg syncconf`, so the live peer goes with the file
  %%> - it runs for every removed Node, also one whose key the Gateway never recorded, because the Node's own enrollment run may have written the file
  %%> - it writes no other peer and keeps none: the intent is a snapshot from admission, and a removal admitted at the same time as another must not put the other's peer back (two removals five seconds apart left a removed VM live on the hub for five minutes)
  SW->>P: Withdraw Router ingress<br/>SSH argv
  SW->>P: Withdraw Metrics<br/>cfgmgmt file · SSH argv
  SW->>P: Withdraw private DNS<br/>native child workflow
  opt routing domains change
    SW->>P: Refresh peer DNS<br/>native child workflow
  end
  %%> Why after the Node:
  %%> - the hub peer is withdrawn after the Node-side cleanup, so the cleanup still has a path
  SW->>SW: Confirm the removal<br/>native assert
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks the fleet no longer lists the Node; it contacts no machine
  %%> Run files:
  %%> - the run folder with the intent, inventory and any secrets is removed after success or failure
  alt every step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Delete the Node record
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Node record and its owned records are deleted, and the removal is broadcast
    %%> - the result lists `roles_shed` and `retained_on_node`; for an unreachable machine it also names the Orbit keys and the worker unit that stay on it
  else a step fails, is cancelled or the budget ends
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    Note over GW: Keep the Node, mark failed
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Node stays with status `failed` and the failed step `remove:operation`, so you can see it; its claimed roles are marked failed too
    %%> - a cancelled or interrupted run ends the same way; later retirement steps do not run
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - finished steps stay; there is no rollback
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again after fixing the cause; every step skips work that is already done
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
  end
